Enclosed is a patch against 2.6.3-bk7 that provides a system call
auditing framework usable by SELinux.  Comments and testing would be
greatly appreciated.

The main goal of this work was to integrate a low-overhead system-call
and logging infrastructure with SELinux.  There are two main parts, one
that is always on (generic logging) and one that you can disable at
boot- or run-time (per-system-call auditing).

Logging:
    1) Uses a netlink socket for communication with user-space.  All
       messages are logged via the netlink socket if a user-space daemon
       is listening.  If not, the messages are logged via printk to the
       syslog daemon (by default).
    2) Messages can be dropped (optionally) based on message rate or
       memory use (this isn't fully integrated into the selinux/avc.c
       part of the patch, but the avc.c code that currently does this
       can be eliminated).
    3) When some part of the kernel generates part of an audit record,
       the partial record is sent immediately to user-space, AND the
       system call "auditable" flag is automatically set for that call
       -- thereby producing extra information at syscall exit (including
       some file names that might make it easier to debug policy -- see
       below).

System-call auditing:
    1) At task-creation time, an audit context is allocated and linked
       off the task structure.
    2) At syscall entry time, if the audit context exists, information
       is filled in (syscall number, timestamp, but not arguments).
    3) During the system call, calls to getname() and path_lookup() are
       intercepted.  These routines are called when the kernel is
       actually looking up information that will be used to make the
       decision about whether the syscall will succeed or fail.  A great
       effort has been made to avoid copying the information that
       getname generates (a pointer is stored instead), since getname is
       already making a kernel-private copy of the information.  [Note
       that storing copies of arguments requires complexity and overhead
       that you arguably don't need.  With this patch, for example, if
       chroot("foo") fails because you are not root, "foo" will not
       appear in the audit record because the kernel determined the
       syscall cannot proceed before it ever needed to look up "foo".]
    4) At syscall exit time, if the "auditable" flag has been set (e.g.,
       because SELinux generated an avc record), the syscall-part of the
       audit record is generated, including file names and inode
       numbers.  Some of this information is currently complementary to
       the information that avc.c generates (e.g., file names and some
       inode numbers), but some is less complete (e.g., getname doesn't
       return a fully-qualified path, and this patch does not add the
       overhead of determining one).  [Note that the complete audit
       record comes to userspace in pieces, which eliminates the need to
       store messages for long periods inside the kernel.]
    5) At task-exit time, the audit context is destroyed.

    At steps 1, 2, and 4, simple filtering can be done (e.g., a heavy
    database role uid might have syscall auditing disabled for
    performance reasons).  The filtering is probably more simple than
    what is needed, so I'd appreciate feedback here.  On the other hand,
    the framework should rely on SELinux to provide most of the
    decisions about whether or not to audit a particular event.

A few (obvious) things are not yet implemented:
    1) Filtering on syscall personality (i.e., i386 calls on x86_64).
    2) Identity tracking (this should be done via the netlink socket,
       similar to how user-space messages (AUDIT_USER) are handled now).
    3) Multi-platform hooks (currently this has only been tested on
       i386, x86_64, and ppc64).
    4) Documentation.

For the brave, I have also attached a small user-space test program and
pseudo-daemon (it logs to stdout).  No man pages, but some online help.
E.g., you can try:
    Window 1:
        ./auditd
    Window 2 (you don't have to have the daemon running to try this, but
    enabled has to be 1):
        ./auditctl -s
        ./auditctl -a entry,always -S open
        ls
        ./auditctl -d entry,always -S open

More recent versions may also be available from:
    http://people.redhat.com/faith/audit/



